fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Now we need to construct a long exact sequence at the top:
\usepackage{mlmodern, tikz, tikz-cd, quiver, mathtools, amssymb, stmaryrd, mathrsfs}
\begin {tikzcd} & {?} \\ {i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r})} && {i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r-1})} \\ & {\pi _n (F_p)} \\ {\pi _n (X_p)} && {\pi _n (X_{p-1})} \arrow [from=1-2, to=2-1] \arrow ["{i}"', from=2-1, to=2-3] \arrow [hook, from=2-1, to=4-1] \arrow [from=2-3, to=1-2] \arrow [hook, from=2-3, to=4-3] \arrow ["k"{description}, from=3-2, to=4-1] \arrow ["i"', from=4-1, to=4-3] \arrow ["j"{description}, from=4-3, to=3-2] \end {tikzcd}
The most straightforward choice is attempting to lift the long exact sequence below. From the perspective of k, we want the question mark to be k^{-1}i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r}). This is sufficient for the bottom-left corner, as the image of this under k is exactly \operatorname {im} k \cap \operatorname {im} i^r = \ker i \cap \operatorname {im} i^r, so this corner is exact.

However, the top-right map cannot simply be j, as j \mathbin {\circ } i = 0, so applying j to the image of i^r is identically zero. With this in mind, the next obvious choice is to have it send i^r(x) to j(x) (i.e. j \mathbin {\circ } i^{-r}), since we cannot have any iteration of i in the argument of j. However, this is not well-defined, and introduces an intertermination of j \ker (i^r : \pi _n (X_{p+r-1}) \to \pi _n (X_{p-1})). To solve this, we simply quotient by that factor in the codomain. (Note that since the triangle isn’t actually closed, but a rolled-up spiral, the indices of j needs a suitable shifting.)
\usepackage{mlmodern, tikz, tikz-cd, quiver, mathtools, amssymb, stmaryrd, mathrsfs}
\begin {tikzcd} & {\mkern -24mu{\dfrac {k^{-1} \operatorname {im}(i^r : \pi _n (X_{p+r}) \to \pi _n (X_p))}{j \ker (i^r : \pi _{n-1} (X_{p-1}) \to \pi _{n-1} (X_{p-r-1}))}}\mkern -24mu} \\ {i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r})} && {i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r-1})} \\ & {\pi _n (F_p)} \\ {\pi _n (X_p)} && {\pi _n (X_{p-1})} \arrow ["k"', from=1-2, to=2-1] \arrow ["{i}"', from=2-1, to=2-3] \arrow [hook, from=2-1, to=4-1] \arrow ["{j i^{-r}}"', from=2-3, to=1-2] \arrow [hook, from=2-3, to=4-3] \arrow ["k"{description}, from=3-2, to=4-1] \arrow ["i"', from=4-1, to=4-3] \arrow ["j"{description}, from=4-3, to=3-2] \end {tikzcd}
This is ok, as k \mathbin {\circ } j = 0, so the quotiented subgroup is already in the kernel of k. We can now check that the triangle is indeed exact.

Removing the unknown components from the diagram, we are left with the following sequence:
\usepackage{mlmodern, tikz, tikz-cd, quiver, mathtools, amssymb, stmaryrd, mathrsfs}
\begin {tikzcd}[column sep=small] & {E_{n,p+1,r}} && {E_{n,p,r}} && {E_{n,p-1,r}} \\ \textcolor {gray}{\cdots } && \textcolor {gray}{{i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r})}} && \textcolor {gray}{{i^r \pi _n (X_{p+r-1})}} && \textcolor {gray}{\cdots } \arrow [color={gray}, from=1-2, to=2-1] \arrow [from=1-4, to=1-2] \arrow ["k"{description}, color={gray}, from=1-4, to=2-3] \arrow [from=1-6, to=1-4] \arrow [color={gray}, from=1-6, to=2-5] \arrow [color={gray}, from=2-1, to=2-3] \arrow [color={gray}, from=2-3, to=1-2] \arrow [color={gray}, from=2-3, to=2-5] \arrow ["{ji^{-r}}"{description}, color={gray}, from=2-5, to=1-4] \arrow [color={gray}, from=2-5, to=2-7] \arrow [color={gray}, from=2-7, to=1-6] \end {tikzcd}
It’s clear that the only maps we can use are the compositions d = j i^{-r} k. Again, since the triangles isn’t actually closed, d sends E_{n, p, r} to E_{n+1, p+r, r} instead of E_{n, p+1, r} as suggested in the diagram. We immediately notice that by exactness of the original triangles, d^2 = j i^{-r} \cancel {k j} i^{-r} k = 0. Therefore E_{n,p,r} forms a chain complex. We can calculate at E_{n, p, r}: \begin {aligned} \ker d &= k^{-1} \{ i^r (x) \mid x \in \pi _n (X_{p+r}), j(x) = 0 \} \\ \operatorname {im} d &= j \{ x \in \pi _{n-1} (X_{p-1}) \mid \exists y, k(y) = i^r(x) \}. \end {aligned} Finally by exactness of the original triangle, \ker j = \operatorname {im} i, and \operatorname {im} k = \ker i. Therefore the homology of d is exactly E_{n,p,r+1}.
